<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ubangi-Shari</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1958 CE · capital Bangui</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony on the Ubangi River that became the Central African Republic, exploited by concession companies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French posts along the Ubangi River, including Bangui founded in 1889, were organised in 1903 into the colony of Ubangi-Shari, separate from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> was attached to it for some years. It formed part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> from 1910.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Concession companies forced people to collect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and later grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and thousands were conscripted to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway">Congo-Ocean railway</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a>. The Kongo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wara">Wara</a> rebellion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gbaya-people">Gbaya</a> from 1928 to 1931 was one of the largest colonial-era revolts. Sultanates such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dar-al-kuti">Dar al Kuti</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-bangassou">Bangassou</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-zemio">Zemio</a> were absorbed into the administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1946 the Catholic priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barthelemy-boganda">Barthélemy Boganda</a> led the nationalist movement. Ubangi-Shari became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> in December 1958 and independent on 13 August 1960, after Boganda's death in an air crash in 1959.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ubangi-shari.jpg" alt="Ubangi-Shari" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bangui</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Barthélemy Boganda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Sango</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oubangui-Chari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=ubangi-shari" aria-label="Ubangi-Shari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5195 2014 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ubangi-Shari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5515 2270L5633 2256M5515 2270L5697 2245M5515 2270L5262 2418M5515 2270L5424 2593M5515 2270L5401 2038"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5633 2256h0M5697 2245h0M5262 2418h0M5424 2593h0M5401 2038h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5515 2270h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5515 2270h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bangui. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=ubangi-shari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
